{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>An altar of earth.</b> So that they cannot make sculptured images." "<b>And you shall sacrifice upon it.</b> Opposite it." "<b>Your burnt-offering and your peace-offerings.</b> Burnt-offering in the north, peace-offerings in the south — from the middle of the altar and northward, like the north; and from the middle of the altar and southward, like the south. And I have only the north of the altar that is valid for service — from where do I derive the rest of the entire north? Scripture says: 'ושחט את הכבש במקום אשר ישחט את החטאת ואת העולה.' There is no need for Scripture to say 'במקום הקודש,' and what does Scripture mean by 'במקום הקודש'? To validate the entire northern side." "<b>And you shall sacrifice upon it.</b> Opposite it. It even refers to an Israelite, for it is established that slaughter is valid by a non-priest, and from receiving the blood onward is a priestly mitzvah. And furthermore, in tractate Yoma, in the first chapter, it implies that a non-priest comes near the altar, and in tractate Sukkah we find that they would set the aravah upright at the side of the altar, and from there all the people would take it and circle the altar — which implies that a non-priest comes near the altar." "<b>In every place.</b> Because the Shekhinah rested in several places, such as Gilgal, Nov, Givon, Shiloh, and Jerusalem. And we learned: a person should always teach his student the short way; therefore it did not specify a defined name anywhere in the Torah, but only in general: 'the place.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:11.032422337Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}